<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/624">624</a>–<a href="/years/692">692</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Ibn al-Zubayr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn al-Zubayr was a son of the Companion al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam and a grandson of Abu Bakr. Refusing to recognise the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a>, he held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and after 683 was proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, recognised for a time across most of the Islamic world during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a>. The Umayyad general al-Hajjaj besieged and bombarded Mecca, and Ibn al-Zubayr was killed fighting in 692.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr.jpg" alt="Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not credited · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_dirham_of_Abd_Allah_ibn_al-Zubayr_690-91.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn al-Zubayr was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in May 624, the eldest son of the Companion Zubayr ibn al-Awwam of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-asad">Banu Asad</a> clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>. His mother Asma was a daughter of Abu Bakr and a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisha">Aisha</a>, and according to ninth-century historians he was the first child born to the Muhajirun, the earliest converts. As a young man he served in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in 647, where he is said to have killed the Byzantine patrician Gregory, and in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 650. He defended the house of the caliph Uthman during the siege of 656 and fought beside Aisha against Ali at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-camel">Battle of the Camel</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ibn al-Zubayr did not oppose Mu'awiya I but in 676 refused to accept his nomination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a> as successor, and rejected Yazid in 680. He escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, where Husayn joined him before Husayn's death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a>. By September 683 he controlled Mecca, calling himself the fugitive at the sanctuary. A Syrian army defeated his allies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> at al-Harra in the summer of 683 and besieged Mecca from 24 September, severely damaging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a>. News of Yazid's death in November led the army to withdraw after Ibn al-Zubayr refused a recognition conditional on ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>He then declared himself commander of the faithful. After the death of Yazid's son Mu'awiya II, Umayyad authority collapsed and most provinces, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, offered allegiance, and coins were struck in his name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a>. His authority outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> stayed largely nominal. Marwan I won at Marj Rahit in July 684 and took Egypt in March 685, and Kharijite gains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> isolated Ibn al-Zubayr further.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 691 Abd al-Malik defeated Mus'ab at Maskin and conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. His commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hajjaj-ibn-yusuf">al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</a> then besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> for six months. Most of Ibn al-Zubayr's followers, including his sons Khubayb and Hamza, surrendered on offers of pardon. On his mother's advice he went into battle and was killed in 692. Al-Hajjaj displayed the body on a gibbet until Abd al-Malik let his mother take it, and it was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. His death ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-fitna">Second Fitna</a>. He had argued that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> should be chosen by consultation among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> and not inherited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. Sources hostile to the Umayyads called him a model of piety, while others criticised him as jealous and harsh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Abd al-Malik (r. 685–705) it recovered the caliphate from the rival Zubayrid claimant <b>Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</b>, and it proved more stable and more inventive in administration than Muawiyah's line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Second Fitna, the Umayyad caliph Marwan I and his Kalb allies defeated the Qays tribes, who backed the Mecca-based caliph <b>Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</b>, north of Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Umayyad siege of Mecca, held by <b>Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</b> against the caliph Yazid I, the Kaaba was severely damaged by fire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-31">31 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</b>, son of a Companion of the Prophet and grandson of Abu Bakr, refused to recognise the Umayyad caliph Yazid I and took refuge in Mecca.</blockquote><a href="/state/caliphate-of-ibn-al-zubayr">Caliphate of Ibn al-Zubayr</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=668" aria-label="Medina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5603 1263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6053 1533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=668">What was here in 668 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
